1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.635
In this notebook, you're going to build
the agent

2
00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:05.205
that's going to be used
throughout the rest of this course.

3
00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:08.208
As you've seen, this is an example agent
that uses a few different tools

4
00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.745
and a router to answer questions
about a data file that's attached.

5
00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.814
So first thing you want to do is import

6
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.817
a few different libraries
that are going to be used by this agent.

7
00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:21.354
So you have an OpenAI
library, Pandas, Json,

8
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:25.358
and then a library called duckDB,
which gets used for some of the SQL

9
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:27.627
code that you're going to run,

10
00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.397
as well as pydantic,
some markdown displaying,

11
00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:34.100
and then some helper methods
that are used to gather the OpenAI key

12
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.536
and a few other kind of things
specific to your environment here.

13
00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:40.573
And then next thing you're going to do
is you'll want to set up

14
00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:44.677
your OpenAI client
that will be used throughout this agent.

15
00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.248
In this case, using GPT-4o-mini.

16
00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:50.984
And then you're ready
to start defining the tools.

17
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.887
So as mentioned there's three tools
that the agent is going to have.

18
00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.856
The first is a database
lookup tool that it will use.

19
00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:00.126
And so there's a local file
in the notebook

20
00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:04.864
that is store sales price
elasticity promotions data file.

21
00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:08.068
So, here's a path to that file
that's going to be used.

22
00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.805
And this is a parquet file
which is just a file that's going to be

23
00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.942
used to simulate the database
that your agent can interact with.

24
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:21.776
And this file contains a bunch of
transaction data of different store sales.

25
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:23.049
So you can imagine every time

26
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.853
there's a sale in a particular store,
one new entry gets added to this file

27
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.723
with the product skew
and with the any promotions

28
00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.928
attached to it, as well as the
price and the cost of that sale.

29
00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.132
So now,
there's a few steps to this first tool.

30
00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:42.302
First, the tool needs to generate SQL

31
00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.905
that can be run on this database
that you have locally.

32
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.976
And then it needs to actually run
that SQL command and get the result back.

33
00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:53.746
So to generate
the SQL you're going to use a prompt here.

34
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.350
So you'll need a prompt
to actually generate the SQL code.

35
00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:01.020
And so you can see that here
generate a SQL query based on a prompt.

36
00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.524
Do not reply with anything
besides the SQL query.

37
00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:08.428
And you can see the prompt
is going to be passed in as a variable,

38
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:12.332
as are the columns for the table
and the table name, so that the LLM knows

39
00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.933
what available columns there are.

40
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.035
What the table is,
which is critical when it creates the

41
00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:17.337
SQL code that's going to run.

42
00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.739
So now you can set

43
00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:23.176
up a method
to actually generate the SQL code itself,

44
00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:26.579
using that GPT-4o-mini model
that you established.

45
00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.650
So here is the generated
SQL method that you might use.

46
00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:34.921
So it takes in a prompt list of columns
and then table name.

47
00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:38.324
And then it formats
this SQL generation prompt

48
00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.327
that you have up here
with those different variables.

49
00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.597
So you'll see the prompt, columns
and table name passed in.

50
00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.300
And then that formatted prompt
will be sent

51
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.870
over to GPT-4o-mini as a user message.

52
00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:56.237
And then that response will get returned.
And now you need to incorporate

53
00:02:56.409 --> 00:03:00.380
that SQL generation code into a tool
that can be used

54
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.550
by the agent to actually run the code
after it's been generated.

55
00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:07.153
And so for that you'll find another method
here.

56
00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.789
In this case, this is going to
be called lookup sales data.

57
00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.493
And so what this method will do
is it takes in a prompt.

58
00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:16.196
And then it will try to first create

59
00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:19.432
the database from that local parquet file.

60
00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:23.937
So you can see it creates a data frame
here using pandas with that local file.

61
00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:27.173
And then it uses the DuckDB library here
to say

62
00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:30.176
create this table
if it doesn't exist already,

63
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.545
which will create a SQL database

64
00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.548
table
based on that data frame that you have.

65
00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.885
And then next, you'll use the generate SQL
query

66
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.888
method that you just created
to create an SQL query here.

67
00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:45.792
And that can then be slightly modified
here to remove any extra

68
00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:49.429
trending white spaces
or any other extra characters here,

69
00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:52.832
and then run
using duckDB to receive a result.

70
00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.070
This step right here can be useful
because oftentimes you'll have

71
00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:01.874
the LLM respond with SQL at the top
to kind of format the response there.

72
00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:03.243
It doesn't just respond with code.

73
00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:05.278
Sometimes it includes that extra SQL bit,

74
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.281
which is why you're removing
some of those characters here.

75
00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:11.251
DuckDB here is used to take

76
00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:14.254
the data frame that you have
and turn it into,

77
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.757
a corner database in memory
that you can access.

78
00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:20.793
And that will make it easier to run
any kinds of,

79
00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:24.597
queries across that database and will work
fairly efficiently and very quickly.

80
00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:28.768
And it also allows you to use generated
SQL code

81
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:32.171
and query that database
using SQL code in a very easy way.

82
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.808
Now that you have your tool defined here,

83
00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.745
it's always good to make sure
that the tool is actually working.

84
00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:42.482
So you can put in an example query here,
print out the result,

85
00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:47.120
say example data and call your method
with show me the sales data

86
00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:52.158
for store 1320 on November 1st, 2021,
and you can run that.

87
00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:55.261
It might take a second to run
and you should get a response back.

88
00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:59.065
Should get a response
back that looks something like this.

89
00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:02.068
So you see data that's been retrieved
from your SQL database.

90
00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.271
Great.
That's one tool down. Two more to go.

91
00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:08.274
So next tool is a data analysis tool.

92
00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:11.544
And so this is a tool
that can take in data and then makes an LLM

93
00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:15.315
call to conduct analysis
and capture trends from that data.

94
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.483
So, similar to before

95
00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.819
you're going to want to have a prompt
for that particular tool.

96
00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.354
In this case, it's pretty straightforward.

97
00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.256
It's analyze the following data.

98
00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:24.924
And there's a variable for data.

99
00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:27.293
And then your job
is to answer the following question.

100
00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.662
And there's a variable for the prompt.

101
00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.632
Now you'll have a method
to actually make that call.

102
00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:35.368
So you can have an analyze sales data
method here

103
00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.870
that takes in a prompt and some data.

104
00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.472
And then similar to before,

105
00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:43.076
you're going to format the prompt
you just created with those parameters,

106
00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:46.346
make a call to your OpenAI client

107
00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:49.315
and get a response back as the analysis.

108
00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:51.851
And then you can always do
some little bit of error

109
00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.986
checking here
to see if the analysis doesn't come back

110
00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.888
for whatever reason,
then you can still return within

111
00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:58.124
no analysis could be generated.

112
00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:00.426
This kind of thing
is especially helpful in agents

113
00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:03.529
because if you have a response
that breaks halfway through your agent,

114
00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.465
you want to make sure that that doesn't
cascade

115
00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:07.233
and break everything else
inside of your agent.

116
00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:08.000
So, good error

117
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.137
checking is almost more important
in agents than it can be in other tools.

118
00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:14.741
And now, with your analyze sales

119
00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:18.311
data method defined, you can test
and make sure that that's working.

120
00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:22.682
So you can call analyze
sales data the prompt:

121
00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:24.617
What trends do you see in this data?

122
00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:27.854
And you can pass in the example data
that you retrieved with the previous tool.

123
00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:30.690
And you should get back
something that looks like this,

124
00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.360
which is some markdown
formatted response from your model.

125
00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:39.198
And moving on to the third and final tool
that the agent will use here.

126
00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:41.968
This third tool
is a data visualization tool.

127
00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.770
And if you think back to the previous
lesson,

128
00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.739
you'll remember that
this works using two different steps.

129
00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:51.043
So at first generates a chart config
or what we're calling a chart config.

130
00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:54.147
And then it will use that chart
config to generate

131
00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:57.116
some Python code
to generate a visualization.

132
00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:00.219
So starting with the chart config here.

133
00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:03.222
You're going to again have a prompt that
you want to use to generate that config.

134
00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:06.426
You'll see it says generate a chart
config based on some data.

135
00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:08.761
Your goal is to show a visualization goal.

136
00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:10.596
And then

137
00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:14.100
here, you're going to use a tool
called pydantic

138
00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:18.571
along with a feature of OpenAI
called Structured Output.

139
00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:22.742
So that you can ensure that when you make
a call to generate a chart config,

140
00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:25.912
it matches a very specific format
that you're defining here.

141
00:07:26.546 --> 00:07:30.283
So first thing to do that is define
the actual format that you want to use.

142
00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:32.418
So in this case this visualization config

143
00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:36.422
is a set of a dictionary
with four different variables here.

144
00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:39.525
Chart type x axis, y axis, and title.

145
00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:40.426
And then you can see there's

146
00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:43.396
a description of what
each of those different variables are.

147
00:07:43.663 --> 00:07:48.367
And you'll use that in just one
sec here with your OpenAI call in order

148
00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:52.004
to make sure that the output
matches that format exactly.

149
00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:54.974
So now you can define a method

150
00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:58.544
for extracting the chart config
and creating that chart config.

151
00:07:59.212 --> 00:08:02.448
It'll take in some data
as well as a visualization goal.

152
00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.518
And then again, it's going to format

153
00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:08.521
the chart
config prompt that you set up above.

154
00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:11.457
And then there's going to be slight
difference in this call that's made here.

155
00:08:11.457 --> 00:08:12.291
Because now you're using

156
00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:16.329
that structured output feature
to tell OpenAI that it should respond in

157
00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:19.432
a format that matches that visualization
config that you just created.

158
00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:23.302
So one important thing to note is there's
a slightly different message string here

159
00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:28.541
to allow you to include that response
format variable for visualization config.

160
00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:30.510
And again
what this will do is it will make it

161
00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:34.046
so that when OpenAI responds,
when GPT-4o-mini responds,

162
00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.984
it will be in the format
that matches your visualization config.

163
00:08:38.518 --> 00:08:41.654
So you'll be able to access things
like the content dot

164
00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:45.391
chart type
or content dot x axis, or y axis, or title.

165
00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:49.729
And the broader goal of this approach
is you're really scoping down

166
00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:50.329
the first thing

167
00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:53.833
you ask the model to do to say
instead of just generating Python code,

168
00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.136
it really just has to pull out, okay,
what's the chart type I should create?

169
00:08:57.136 --> 00:09:00.373
What's the x axis, y axis and title
that I should give to my chart?

170
00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:04.010
And then you can pass that into an LLM
in the next step of this tool.

171
00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:08.180
Do you have a more defined goal
for that LLM to generate code for.

172
00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:13.085
So moving on to the next step within
this third tool

173
00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:17.290
is to actually create the chart code
itself.

174
00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:20.293
So similar to before, you're going
to create a prompt to start off with.

175
00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:23.829
It says write Python code
to generate a chart based on this config.

176
00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:26.132
And it's passing in this config here.

177
00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:29.302
And then you can define a method

178
00:09:29.302 --> 00:09:33.139
to use that particular prompt
to generate Python code.

179
00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:37.143
So you'll see this create chart
method here takes in a dictionary

180
00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:38.177
which is that config.

181
00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:40.846
So that's what it was
generated by the last step there.

182
00:09:40.846 --> 00:09:43.849
And then it formats
your create chart prompt here.

183
00:09:43.983 --> 00:09:48.888
With that config. Makes your standard
OpenAI call gets its response back.

184
00:09:49.522 --> 00:09:52.825
And then similar to what you did
with the SQL code, it's always a good idea

185
00:09:52.825 --> 00:09:57.096
to make sure that, any sort of prefix here
that gets added by the model like this

186
00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:58.397
Python one you see here,

187
00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:01.534
is getting removed and replaced so that
you're only left with a runnable code.

188
00:10:02.635 --> 00:10:04.170
And now the last thing to do with
this tool

189
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.139
is just compose those two different steps
together into one method.

190
00:10:07.974 --> 00:10:09.375
So now this generate visualization

191
00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:12.745
method is going to take in some data
and a visualization goal.

192
00:10:13.346 --> 00:10:18.250
It's first going to create a config
with the extract chart config method.

193
00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:22.321
And then it will create some Python code
using the create chart code.

194
00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:25.825
And finally, you can test and make sure
that this is actually all

195
00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:27.059
working correctly.

196
00:10:27.059 --> 00:10:30.496
So you can say generate visualization
with some example data

197
00:10:30.763 --> 00:10:32.765
and then a visualization goal.

198
00:10:32.765 --> 00:10:34.734
In this case, that's similar to the prompt

199
00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:36.302
the user might have.

200
00:10:36.302 --> 00:10:39.305
And we'll print out the response as well.

201
00:10:39.372 --> 00:10:42.575
And you should now see some Python code
that's been printed out here

202
00:10:43.142 --> 00:10:46.145
that will generate
a particular visualization for you.

203
00:10:46.712 --> 00:10:49.849
And you can actually take it
one step further and run the code here

204
00:10:49.849 --> 00:10:52.852
using the command execute code.

205
00:10:52.985 --> 00:10:54.820
And you'll see a visualization.

206
00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.722
In this case it looks like
the code was created correctly.

207
00:10:56.722 --> 00:10:58.758
But the visualization
looks slightly off here.

208
00:10:58.758 --> 00:11:02.061
So, this is where evaluation
is going to come into play later on

209
00:11:02.061 --> 00:11:03.496
because you'll be able to catch cases
where

210
00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:05.398
maybe the visualization is slightly off.

211
00:11:06.632 --> 00:11:08.167
One important note is that

212
00:11:08.167 --> 00:11:12.071
you always have the option to include
the ability for your agent to run code,

213
00:11:12.238 --> 00:11:13.973
but it's something you have to be
a little bit careful with

214
00:11:13.973 --> 00:11:18.711
because if you just run code that's been
created by an LLM, it could have errors.

215
00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:20.479
It could do things that you don't expect
it to.

216
00:11:20.479 --> 00:11:24.417
So it's always good to run code
within an enclosed environment

217
00:11:24.417 --> 00:11:26.652
if you're giving your agent
that capability.

218
00:11:26.652 --> 00:11:30.589
In this example agent, you left that
capability out just to keep things safe.

219
00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:31.691
But something good to know

220
00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:34.694
if you're incorporating the ability
to run code into future agents.

221
00:11:35.961 --> 00:11:39.999
Now, with all of your tools defined,
the next step is to put those tools

222
00:11:39.999 --> 00:11:43.002
into a format
that can be understood by your router.

223
00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:46.439
In this case, you're using an OpenAI,
GPT-4o-mini

224
00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:48.541
router with function calling.

225
00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:52.411
So there's a very specific format that
OpenAI expects to be pass those tools in.

226
00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.814
You can add that in here.

227
00:11:54.814 --> 00:11:57.817
And what you'll see is that each object
within this array of tools

228
00:11:58.184 --> 00:12:01.187
has a specific Json format
that's been defined.

229
00:12:01.253 --> 00:12:04.523
So in this first one, this first tool here
is your lookup sales data tool.

230
00:12:04.523 --> 00:12:06.892
So this is a method
that you defined earlier.

231
00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:09.895
And you'll see there's a description
in here of what that tool does.

232
00:12:09.929 --> 00:12:12.932
Or it can do lookup data
from this particular file.

233
00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:16.102
And then you'll see
that certain properties have been defined

234
00:12:16.102 --> 00:12:20.272
for things like the prompt parameter
that needs to be passed into this tool.

235
00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:24.510
And so this is what tells the router
how to choose this function.

236
00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:28.614
And if it chooses this function to call
what parameters need to be passed into it.

237
00:12:29.115 --> 00:12:30.883
So these descriptions are really critical

238
00:12:30.883 --> 00:12:34.587
both for the description of the function
as well as descriptions of the parameters.

239
00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.023
Because if you get these wrong, the router
may choose not to call your function.

240
00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:40.392
It may not understand
what that function does,

241
00:12:40.392 --> 00:12:43.129
or it may think the function can do things
that it actually can't do.

242
00:12:43.129 --> 00:12:45.064
So this is often
something that you end up changing

243
00:12:45.064 --> 00:12:48.067
a lot of times when building an agent
is getting the description

244
00:12:48.067 --> 00:12:50.936
and the parameter
descriptions exactly right.

245
00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:53.939
So there'll be an entry here
for each of your different tools.

246
00:12:54.006 --> 00:12:56.442
As well as a mapping at the bottom here

247
00:12:56.442 --> 00:13:00.179
to say this name here
matches this particular function, which

248
00:13:00.212 --> 00:13:01.046
I got used in a second.

249
00:13:02.348 --> 00:13:02.715
Awesome.

250
00:13:02.715 --> 00:13:05.718
You're now ready to define your router.

251
00:13:05.718 --> 00:13:08.854
To define your router
you can use a few different approaches.

252
00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:11.590
Let's walk through the code here.

253
00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:15.728
So in this case, this run agent function
that you have it's going to be

254
00:13:15.728 --> 00:13:17.062
what runs your router.

255
00:13:17.062 --> 00:13:20.166
Oftentimes, people will set up routers
to either work recursively

256
00:13:20.166 --> 00:13:23.302
where you continually call a function
that represents your router.

257
00:13:23.903 --> 00:13:26.539
Or in this case, you could go for something
even simpler and just use

258
00:13:26.539 --> 00:13:29.675
a while loop here that you break out of
when the agent completes its task.

259
00:13:30.276 --> 00:13:33.512
Looking through the code that you see
here, you have the messages

260
00:13:33.512 --> 00:13:36.515
object
that's passed into this function here.

261
00:13:36.515 --> 00:13:37.716
And then first

262
00:13:37.716 --> 00:13:41.387
we'll make sure that that messages object
matches the right format that we expect.

263
00:13:42.087 --> 00:13:44.824
So in this case
you want to have it as a user message.

264
00:13:44.824 --> 00:13:47.827
And this is a format that's expected
by OpenAI in this case.

265
00:13:48.093 --> 00:13:52.131
So you want to check for cases
where maybe you've passed in just a string

266
00:13:52.131 --> 00:13:55.935
as opposed to a dictionary of
these messages and correct for that case.

267
00:13:56.869 --> 00:13:59.038
And then if the system prompt

268
00:13:59.038 --> 00:14:01.106
has not actually been added
into this already,

269
00:14:01.106 --> 00:14:02.975
then you'll want to make sure
that that's been added.

270
00:14:02.975 --> 00:14:05.611
We'll define
that system prompt in just one moment.

271
00:14:05.611 --> 00:14:09.315
And then to actually run your agent,
it's going to follow a similar loop here

272
00:14:09.415 --> 00:14:14.119
where you first make a call to OpenAI
with your tools object

273
00:14:14.119 --> 00:14:17.122
that you just set up up there,
as well as the messages,

274
00:14:17.223 --> 00:14:19.758
and then you'll receive a response back

275
00:14:19.758 --> 00:14:22.862
to look for if there's any tool
calls inside that response.

276
00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:24.797
And then if

277
00:14:24.797 --> 00:14:27.800
there are, you're
gonna want to handle those tool calls.

278
00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.803
So you'll need to define
what that means in just a second.

279
00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.806
And then if there's no more tool calls
then you can return the response

280
00:14:33.806 --> 00:14:35.608
back to the user.

281
00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:38.611
So there's a couple more pieces
you need here for the router. First,

282
00:14:38.878 --> 00:14:40.779
you need this system prompt variable.

283
00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:42.948
So your router needs
some sort of system prompt.

284
00:14:42.948 --> 00:14:45.851
So you can define that
a very basic version of this is just

285
00:14:45.851 --> 00:14:49.455
you are a helpful assistant that can
answer questions about this data set.

286
00:14:50.656 --> 00:14:52.591
And the last piece you'll need is

287
00:14:52.591 --> 00:14:55.594
a way to handle the tool
calls that get made by your router.

288
00:14:55.895 --> 00:14:58.898
And so you can add in a method
to handle those tool calls.

289
00:14:59.298 --> 00:15:02.768
So here this is just looping through
each tool call that gets made.

290
00:15:03.369 --> 00:15:07.273
And then for that tool call
looking up the corresponding function name

291
00:15:08.073 --> 00:15:11.076
parsing out any arguments
that have been included in the tool call,

292
00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:14.380
and then calling that function
with those arguments

293
00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:17.683
and appending the result
back to the messages object.

294
00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:20.786
One other important thing to note here
is that OpenAI

295
00:15:20.786 --> 00:15:23.789
specifically,
as well as some other models,

296
00:15:24.023 --> 00:15:27.526
rely on a behavior where if they tell you
to call a particular tool,

297
00:15:27.526 --> 00:15:31.063
if that's the response you get back
from your router, then in the next call

298
00:15:31.063 --> 00:15:35.067
that you make to them, you need to include
a response of that particular tool.

299
00:15:35.401 --> 00:15:37.770
So they'll give you a tool call ID,

300
00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:39.038
and if you don't include back

301
00:15:39.038 --> 00:15:42.374
a tool message here
with that particular tool call ID,

302
00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:47.079
then they'll actually error and say,
hey, you need to call this particular tool

303
00:15:47.079 --> 00:15:50.082
and you need to give me a response for
that tool call that I asked you to make.

304
00:15:50.482 --> 00:15:53.719
And now with each of these
you are ready to go and run your agent.

305
00:15:54.553 --> 00:15:56.155
Pull in an example here.

306
00:15:56.155 --> 00:16:01.155
You can ask it a question like "show
me the code for a graph of sales by store.

307
00:16:01.427 --> 00:16:03.929
In November 2021
and tell me what trends you see."

308
00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:06.899
And you'll see it start to run,

309
00:16:06.899 --> 00:16:08.567
and you'll start to get
some print statements

310
00:16:08.567 --> 00:16:11.904
from your router there
where it's making calls to OpenAI,

311
00:16:12.204 --> 00:16:15.274
it receives some tool calls back,
it's going to process those tool calls.

312
00:16:15.274 --> 00:16:17.343
Then go back to the router
and you'll see that

313
00:16:17.343 --> 00:16:19.478
loop complete a few times as the code
runs

314
00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:20.746
here.

315
00:16:20.746 --> 00:16:23.849
And once you see a message saying that
there's no more tool calls to make

316
00:16:23.849 --> 00:16:28.620
and it's returned, the final response,
you can print out your result.

317
00:16:29.688 --> 00:16:31.657
And you should see in this case

318
00:16:31.657 --> 00:16:34.660
some code to generate that graph.

319
00:16:36.028 --> 00:16:39.565
As well as some of the trends that were
gathered by the data analysis tool.

320
00:16:40.733 --> 00:16:41.333
Congratulations.

321
00:16:41.333 --> 00:16:43.936
You now have a working agent
that can answer questions

322
00:16:43.936 --> 00:16:47.039
based on this local database
of sales data that you captured.

